      "text": "The moment of inertia to the center of mass:\n\n1 2 2 2 \uf03d \uf02d \uf02d \uf02d \uf0b4 \uf0d7 \uf02d \uf0d7 \uf02d \uf02d J J 883.92( d d ) 1.67 x x 1.67 x (68.2 d 0.5 ) x \uf02d \uf02d cm corked cm normal normal corked normal 12\n\nThe moment of inertia to the pivot point:\n\n1 2 2 \uf03d \uf02d \uf0b4 \uf0d7 \uf02d \uf0d7 \uf02d J J 1.67 x x 1.67 x (68.2 0.5 ) x \uf02d \uf02d o corked o normal 12\n\n4.2.4. Swing speed:\n\nBeing lighter and the moment of inertia of it getting smaller, a bat is more easy to swing. So, a swing speed gets higher, which will necessarily lead to the increase of the batted ball velocity.\n\nThe swing speed can be expressed according to the empirical formula[10] :\n\nM v \uf03d\uf02d \uf02b corked 75 44.72 67.5\n\nWhere\n\nv is the swing speed (the speed at the end of a swung bat)\n\nis the mass of a corked bat. M corked\n\nThe angular speed of the bat equals:\n\nv w \uf03d 1 68.2\n\n4.3. Parameter determination\n\nIn order to research the influence the length of cork lays on the bat properties, we change the value of the x to see the fluctuation of the Sweet Spot effect.\n\nWhen x=0,5,10,15,20cm, we calculate the following parameters:\n\n4.3.1. Mass of the bat:\n\nTable 6\n\nLength of cork 0 5 10 15 20\n\nMass of bat 883.92 875.57 867.22 858.87 850.52\n\n4.3.2. Length from the center of mass to the pivot\n\nTable 7\n\nLength of cork 0 5 10 15 20\n\nLength from center 44.89 44.69 44.54 44.43 44.37 of mass to the pivot\n\nJ )\n\n4.3.3. Moment of inertia to the center of mass (\n\no\n\nTable 8\n\nLength of cork 0 5 10 15 20\n\nJ 331590 327920 325740 324670 324320 o\n\n4.3.4. Swing speed\n\nTable 9\n\nLength of cork 0 5 10 15 20\n\nSwing speed 40.59 40.67 40.75 40.84 40.92\n\n4.4. Solution and analysis\n\nWe substitute the parameters in our basic model with all the above calculated values, getting solutions as follows:\n\n4.4.1. Influence on the location of the Sweet Spot\n\nTable 10\n\nLength of 0 5 10 15 20 cork\n\nb 49.99 49.89 49.81 49.76 49.73\n\n49.15\n\n",
